.TH EUCLID-CLI-EES-LIST-SUBSCRIPTIONS 1 "September 2026" "euclid-cli 1.0.53" "Euclid CLI Manual"
.SH NAME
euclid-cli-ees-list-subscriptions \- show a subscriber's subscriptions and backlog
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B euclid-cli ees list-subscriptions
.BR \-\-name | \-n
.I NAME
.SH DESCRIPTION
Shows what
.I NAME
is subscribed to \- its event types, filters and account \- together with
.IR waiting ,
the number of events currently held for it, claimed or not.
.PP
.I waiting
is the number to watch. A backlog that only grows is the sign of a
consumer that receives without acknowledging, or one that stopped
running while its subscription stayed behind; in the second case the
events are being kept for something that will never come for them, and
.BR euclid-cli-ees-unsubscribe-events (1)
is what clears them.
.PP
.I lastSeen
is when the subscriber last claimed anything, which distinguishes those
two cases: a consumer that is running but never acknowledges keeps
moving it forward.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-name " " \fINAME\fR
Subscriber name. Required.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
The call succeeded. Subscriptions and the waiting count are printed as
JSON; a name that is not subscribed to anything prints an empty list
rather than failing.
.TP
.B 1
Missing or invalid arguments, no session, or the server rejected the
request.
.SH EXAMPLES
.RS
.nf
euclid-cli ees list-subscriptions --name invoice-import
.fi
.RE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR euclid-cli-ees (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees-subscribe-events (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees-unsubscribe-events (1),
.BR euclid-cli-ees-receive-events (1)
